# ReqLLM Livebook Tools

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:req_llm, github: "agentjido/req_llm"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.14"}
])
```

## Select a provider and model

To use a local ReqLLM checkout, replace the ReqLLM entry in Setup with
`{:req_llm, path: "/absolute/path/to/req_llm"}`.

For Anthropic or OpenAI, add `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` or `OPENAI_API_KEY` to Livebook
secrets and grant this notebook access. For LM Studio, start its API server and
download a text model. If the server requires a token, grant access to the
`LMSTUDIO_API_KEY` secret. The picker uses the secret for the selected provider.

```elixir
alias ReqLLM.Kino, as: Notebook
alias ReqLLM.Kino.{Comparison, Image, ModelPicker, Prompt}

provider_input = ModelPicker.provider_input()
```

Select a provider, then run the next cell to show its models. Run the cell again
after you change the provider or download a model in LM Studio. The LM Studio
server URL is relative to the Livebook runtime. For a server at a different
address, pass `base_url: "http://your-server:1234/v1"` to `ModelPicker.new/2`.

```elixir
picker = ModelPicker.new(Kino.Input.read(provider_input))
ModelPicker.view(picker)
```

Select a model from the list or enter its ID. A manual ID takes precedence. The
LM Studio server can also list embedding models. Choose a model that generates text.

```elixir
model =
  case ModelPicker.read(picker) do
    {:ok, model} -> model
    {:error, message} -> Kino.interrupt!(:error, message)
  end

Kino.Text.new("Selected model #{model.provider}:#{model.id}")
```

## Send a text request

Run this cell to show the form for your selected model. Click **Run** to send
the prompt. The selected provider can charge for the request. In a shared
notebook, all viewers can see the results.

```elixir
prompt_form = Prompt.new(model, default_prompt: "Explain recursion in one sentence.")
Notebook.view(prompt_form)
```

After the response appears, run this cell to read the response. Run it again
after each submission to get the latest result.

```elixir
case Notebook.result(prompt_form) do
  {:ok, response} -> ReqLLM.Response.text(response)
  status -> status
end
```

## Generate an image

For Google or xAI, grant the notebook access to `GOOGLE_API_KEY` or `XAI_API_KEY`.
For Azure OpenAI, grant access to `AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY` and pass the resource's
`base_url` and `deployment` in the form's `:request_options`.

```elixir
image_provider_input = ModelPicker.provider_input(operation: :image)
```

Select a provider and run the next cell.

```elixir
image_picker = ModelPicker.new(Kino.Input.read(image_provider_input), operation: :image)
ModelPicker.view(image_picker)
```

Select an image model and run the next cell. Click **Generate** to send the prompt.
The form shows the images. Use `Notebook.result(image_form)` to get the response.

```elixir
image_model =
  case ModelPicker.read(image_picker) do
    {:ok, model} -> model
    {:error, message} -> Kino.interrupt!(:error, message)
  end

image_form = Image.new(image_model, default_prompt: "A blue cube on a white table")
Notebook.view(image_form)
```

## Compare text models

Set the model list to the models you want to compare. The submit button shows
how many requests it sends. The results include elapsed time, token usage, and
cost when the data is available. Use `Notebook.result(comparison_form)` to get
the rows for further analysis.

```elixir
comparison_form =
  Comparison.new(["openai:gpt-4o-mini", "anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5"],
    default_prompt: "Explain recursion in one sentence."
  )

Notebook.view(comparison_form)
```
